The problem asks us to find a positive integer $N$ such that $N! = X$, given an integer $X$.
The constraints on $X$ are $2 \leq X \leq 3 \times 10^{18}$.
We are guaranteed that such an $N$ exists and is unique.

Let's look at the growth of factorials.
$1! = 1$
$2! = 2$
$3! = 6$
$4! = 24$
$5! = 120$
$6! = 720$
$7! = 5040$
$8! = 40320$
$9! = 362880$
$10! = 3628800$
$11! = 39916800$
$12! = 479001600$
$13! = 6227020800$
$14! = 87178291200$
$15! = 1307674368000$
$16! = 20922789888000$
$17! = 355687428096000$
$18! = 6402373705728000$
$19! = 121645100408832000$
$20! = 2432902008176640000$
$21! = 51090942171709440000$

Notice that $20! \approx 2.4 \times 10^{18}$ and $21! \approx 5.1 \times 10^{19}$.
The maximum value of $X$ is $3 \times 10^{18}$.
Since $21! > 3 \times 10^{18}$, the maximum possible value for $N$ is 20.
Wait, let's double check.
$20! = 2,432,902,008,176,640,000$.
$3 \times 10^{18} = 3,000,000,000,000,000,000$.
So $20! < 3 \times 10^{18}$.
$21! = 21 \times 20! \approx 21 \times 2.43 \times 10^{18} \approx 51 \times 10^{18} = 5.1 \times 10^{19}$.
So indeed, for $X \le 3 \times 10^{18}$, $N$ will be at most 20.

Since $N$ is very small (at most 20), we can simply precompute factorials or compute them on the fly and check which one equals $X$.
Alternatively, since $N$ is small, we can iterate $N$ starting from 1, computing $N!$, and checking if it equals $X$. If it exceeds $X$, we stop (though the problem guarantees a solution exists).

